## 4. Primary Key vs Unique Key

### The Most Important Difference

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│         PRIMARY KEY      │      UNIQUE KEY         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Cannot be NULL           │ Can be NULL             │
│ Only 1 per table         │ Multiple per table      │
│ Automatically indexed    │ May or may not be indexed│
│ Uniquely identifies row  │ Just prevents duplicates│
│ Used for relationships   │ Used for uniqueness     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Detailed Comparison

### Example 1: Basic Difference

```sql
CREATE TABLE employee (
    emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY,          -- Cannot be NULL, must be unique
    email VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE,         -- Can be NULL, must be unique
    phone VARCHAR(10) UNIQUE          -- Can be NULL, must be unique
);

-- Valid insertions:
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'ravi@gmail.com', '9876543210');
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (2, 'puja@gmail.com', NULL);        -- NULL email allowed
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (3, NULL, '9988776655');            -- NULL phone allowed

-- Invalid insertions:
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'new@gmail.com', '9111111111'); -- Duplicate primary key
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (4, 'ravi@gmail.com', '9222222222');-- Duplicate email
```

### Example 2: NULL Handling

```sql
CREATE TABLE student (
    ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    aadhar_number VARCHAR(12) UNIQUE  -- Optional field
);

-- This will FAIL
INSERT INTO student VALUES (NULL, '123456789012');
-- Error: PRIMARY KEY cannot be NULL

-- This will SUCCEED (multiple NULLs in UNIQUE allowed)
INSERT INTO student VALUES (1, NULL);
INSERT INTO student VALUES (2, NULL);
INSERT INTO student VALUES (3, NULL);

SELECT * FROM student;
/*
ID | aadhar_number
1  | NULL
2  | NULL
3  | NULL
*/
```

### Example 3: Real-World Scenario

```sql
-- Student database
CREATE TABLE student (
    student_id INT PRIMARY KEY,           -- Must have, uniquely identifies student
    email VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE NOT NULL,    -- Email is unique for login
    phone VARCHAR(10) UNIQUE,             -- Phone is optional but unique if provided
    aadhar VARCHAR(12) UNIQUE,            -- Optional government ID
    name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL
);

INSERT INTO student VALUES
(1, 'ravi@college.edu', '9876543210', '123456789012', 'Ravi Kumar'),
(2, 'puja@college.edu', '9988776655', NULL, 'Puja Singh'),           -- No Aadhar yet
(3, 'akash@college.edu', NULL, '987654321098', 'Akash Sharma');      -- Will update phone later
```

